---
// Story 1-6. THE main service page.
//
// ⚠️ THE SLUG DOES NOT CHANGE (D4, Keegan: "agreed").
// The Chiropractic & Movement reframe is delivered by the title, the h1 and the
// content. This is the deepest-content page on the site and it has whatever
// ranking history the site has; renaming it for a cosmetic gain risks that for
// nothing. /services/mobility-coaching/ 301s HERE (story 1-7) — this page
// absorbs it.
//
// 🔴 THE FAQ BLOCK BELOW IS PORTED VERBATIM AND IS OUT OF REWRITE SCOPE.
// It is the most citation-shaped asset the site owns. Anyone MAY ADD a question;
// nobody may reword an existing one. The questions now live in src/data/faq.mjs
// (shared with the home page); the merge decision and its owner are documented
// there.
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BookCta from '../../components/BookCta.astro';
import WhatsInvolved from '../../components/WhatsInvolved.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import { service } from '../../lib/schema.mjs';
import { SITE_URL } from '../../lib/site-env.mjs';
import business from '../../data/business.json' with { type: 'json' };
// ST-62 H11 / LN-19 — the FAQ moved to ONE source so the home page can reuse
// four of these questions verbatim without a second hand-copied array.
import { TREATMENT_FAQ } from '../../data/faq.mjs';
import assessing from '../../assets/site/Keegan-Photos-31.jpg';
import adjusting from '../../assets/site/Rib-adjust.jpg';
import fold from '../../assets/site/IMG_1604-scaled.jpg';
// Story 1-16 hero ground. Hands-on soft-tissue work, distinct from the three
// pictures already used as real content further down this page.
import heroSoftTissue from '../../assets/site/glute-massage-scaled.jpg';

const LAST_CHANGED = new Date('2026-07-31T00:00:00+10:00');

// Service schema for the three bookable one-to-one types. Group class and
// handstand class are NOT here — they belong to their own pages, and they carry
// no offers node because Cliniko has no billable item for them (G-8).
const SERVICE_NODES = ['new-patient', 'existing-patient', 'movement-plan-analysis'].map((slug) =>
  service(SITE_URL, slug),
);


---

<BaseLayout
  title={`Chiropractic & Mobility Treatment, Wheeler Heights | ${business.name}`}
  description="Hands-on chiropractic care for pain, stiffness and dysfunction, plus personalised mobility coaching. 30-minute appointments, joint adjustments, muscle release and a rehab plan built from your assessment."
  updated={LAST_CHANGED}
>
  {SERVICE_NODES.map((node) => (
    <script slot="head-jsonld" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(node)} />
  ))}

  {/* Story 1-16. The inner-page hero. The photograph is blurred and dimmed
      into a ground rather than shown as a picture, which is what makes the
      orange headline legible over it — see the measured note on .hero in
      BaseLayout. Decorative in that state, hence empty alt + aria-hidden. */}
  {/* Crop aimed at the work, not the midriff — Keegan 2026-08-06, "still too
      zoomed in". The centred strip landed on a grey t-shirt and a towel; the
      face, the elbow going into the glute and the muscular-system poster all
      sat above it. See the --ml-hero-focus note in BaseLayout for why the
      strip cannot be made WIDER, only aimed. */}
  <section class="hero hero--page" style="--ml-hero-focus: 35%">
    <Image
      src={heroSoftTissue}
      alt=""
      aria-hidden="true"
      widths={[768, 1280, 1920]}
      sizes="100vw"
      loading="eager"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <h1>Chiropractic &amp; Movement</h1>
      {/* ST-62 T2, Star's recommended option of three. It puts his most
          concrete differentiator — the clock — into the first thing anyone
          reads, and carries "the pain is the clue, not the cause" without the
          jargon. It also drops "most patients need fewer visits overall", which
          is a comparison against other practitioners sitting in a hero; Star
          flagged it as an AHPRA s.133 question and this was the
          highest-exposure of its three instances. The other two are recorded in
          her findings for Keegan's own read, and are NOT touched here. */}
      <p class="lead">
        Hands-on care for pain and stiffness, 30 minutes rather than 10, and a plan for why it
        happened in the first place.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Story 1-16 inner-page rhythm: the target follows its hero with a
      two-column image-and-text block on a tinted ground. Here it carries the
      question the page exists to answer, with the assessment photograph that
      was previously stacked above it as a lone column image. */}
  <section class="section section--wide section--sage">
    <div class="split">
      <Image
        src={assessing}
        alt="Keegan assessing a patient's hip and knee range of motion on a treatment table, anatomy charts on the wall behind"
        widths={[480, 960]}
        sizes="(max-width: 40rem) 100vw, 45vw"
      />
      <div>
        <h2>What does an appointment involve?</h2>
        {/* ST-62 T3 — shared with the home page's H10. Edit the words in
            WhatsInvolved.astro, never here. */}
        <WhatsInvolved />
        <BookCta slug="new-patient" label="Book an initial consultation" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* T4, Keegan 2026-08-06: "change the image under 'what is chiropractic
      care' for variety. Landscape is probably better; if it stays portrait,
      put it ALONGSIDE the text rather than above."

      This is the second half of his instruction, which needs no new photograph
      — the picture now sits beside its three paragraphs instead of stacked
      under them, and .split's height cap keeps it from towering (see H9).
      Text first in source order, so the image lands on the RIGHT and
      alternates against the split higher up this page, and so the heading
      still leads when it stacks on a phone.

      🔴 THE IMAGE ITSELF IS STILL HIS CALL AND HAS NOT BEEN CHANGED. His
      instinct about variety has a concrete cause: Rib-adjust.jpg is on the
      home page too, in the "Who is this for?" split, so a reader arriving here
      from home meets the same photograph twice. Unused clinical alternatives
      already in the library: C-spine-tx, cranial-palpation-2, wrist-tx,
      seated-C-spine-test-2, Psoas-test-2, Supine-lat-rom-scaled — all 0.67
      portrait, so any of them drops in without touching this layout. There is
      no unused LANDSCAPE clinical shot, which is why his first preference
      needs a photograph he has not supplied. */}
  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <h2>What is chiropractic?</h2>
      <p>
        Chiropractic is a combination of two Greek words that translates as ‘Done By Hand’.
        Chiropractic care is a hands-on, drug-free approach to health that focuses on the nervous,
        skeletal and muscular systems and how they relate to overall wellbeing. When the joints of
        your spine and body aren’t moving the way they should, it can result in tension, pain,
        discomfort and reduced performance – and that’s where chiropractic comes in.
      </p>
      <p>
        Using gentle, targeted adjustments, chiropractic care works to restore proper movement,
        reduce pain, and support your body’s natural ability to heal. Whether you’re dealing with a
        niggling back problem, recovering from an injury, managing a sports load, or simply want to
        stay on top of your health – chiropractic can play a valuable role at every stage of life,
        for every kind of body.
      </p>
      <p>
        I blend the best of clinical evidence with a whole-body perspective, so you’re never just
        treated as a set of symptoms. I take the time to understand what’s going on, explain what I
        find, and work with you toward lasting results – not just short-term relief.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Image
      src={adjusting}
      alt="Keegan performing a hands-on thoracic adjustment on a patient lying face down"
      widths={[480, 800]}
      sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 100vw, 24rem"
    />
  </div>

  <h2>Why am I different?</h2>
  <p>
    I see every patient for 30-minute appointments, and I treat the whole picture rather than the
    spine alone. Traditional chiropractic tends to follow a pretty set formula – short appointments,
    high frequency, and a heavy focus on spinal adjustments. Many chiropractors see patients for as
    little as 10 minutes, expect multiple visits a week early on, and then keep you coming back
    indefinitely for "maintenance care." And in those appointments, it’s usually just the adjustment
    – a quick pop and crack, and you’re done.
  </p>
  <p>
    I do things differently, because I believe you can’t do a proper job in 10 minutes. In that time,
    I’m not just working on your spine – I look at the whole picture. If your shoulder, hip, or knee
    is part of the problem, we address it. I combine spinal and joint adjustments with hands-on
    muscle release and massage, because muscles and joints work together and ignoring one while
    treating the other only gets you so far. I also place a big emphasis on specific, targeted
    rehabilitation exercises based on what I actually find in your assessment – not a generic sheet
    handed to everyone.
  </p>
  <p>
    The result of this more thorough approach is that most of my patients need fewer visits overall
    and get better faster. My goal is to get you out of my clinic and back to your life – not to keep
    you coming back indefinitely.
  </p>

  {/* The fold. /services/mobility-coaching/ 301s here (story 1-7), so its
      content lives on this page rather than being lost.

      LN-6 / 1-15 section rhythm. The page's one band, and it lands here for a
      reason rather than by elimination: this block IS the retired mobility
      page, so giving it a surface says "this is the other service" — which is
      the one thing a reader who arrived from the old URL needs to understand.

      ⚠️ NOT the pricing block, which is what the placement rule would otherwise
      pick as the practical answer. It carries three BookCta buttons, and orange
      on sage is 2.11:1 — the pairing the brand contract rules out. Banding it
      would have meant re-deriving the button fill, its hover and its focus ring
      against a second ground, and it is the highest-stakes element on the site
      (UD-21 settled it at a 19px/700 FLOOR with 0.22 of headroom). Not worth
      spending that to tint a box. This page is also the longest on the site at
      ~7 screens, so the rhythm work here is the mid-page image plus this band. */}
  <section class="band">
    <h2>Why does mobility matter if I am not in pain?</h2>
    <p>
      Because restricted range of motion is what puts most people back in pain later. As a
      chiropractor, I work with people every day whose pain, dysfunction or performance limitations
      stem from restricted range of motion (and strength within their available range of motion).
      Addressing this in the treatment room is one thing, but what my patients often need to stay out
      of pain over the long term is a dedicated plan to build lasting and functional mobility.
    </p>
    <p>
      Beginning with a thorough flexibility and mobility analysis, I’ll map out precisely where your
      restrictions are and why, then build you a detailed, personalised program to systematically
      address them. From there, regular check-ins keep your progress on track and your plan evolving as
      you do.
    </p>
    <p>
      A word on what the goal actually is. For most people it is not yoga flexibility or gymnast
      flexibility, and it does not need to be. Improving the few areas that are genuinely holding you
      back is what changes how you move day to day. If elite flexibility is what you are after, that
      is a longer project, and I can help with that too.
    </p>
    {/* LN-6 / 1-15 editorial pass. This section absorbed the whole of the retired
        /services/mobility-coaching/ page (story 1-7) and had no picture of anyone
        moving — two clinic photographs above it, then 600 words about mobility
        illustrated by nothing. Landscape on purpose: two portraits already sit
        above, and a third would make the page one column of tall plates. */}
    <Image
      src={fold}
      alt="Keegan in a standing forward fold, knees straight and both palms flat on the floor beside his feet"
      widths={[704, 1408]}
      sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 100vw, 44rem"
      class="page-img"
    />

    <p>
      This service is naturally integrated into the care of all my clients. If you come to me with no
      pain or injuries, we focus on this side of care. If you are in pain or have an injury, we will
      focus more on using regular chiropractic care to address it then naturally progress you to
      functional mobility goals. Either way, you’re getting something that a generic yoga or pilates
      class simply can’t offer – a plan specifically built for your body and your goals that meets you
      at the right point in your movement health journey.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2>What will it cost, and what do I get?</h2>

  <article class="consult">
    <h3>Initial consultation – 1 hour, $120</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Who is this for?</strong> If you suffer from long or short term pain or tension in your
      body and want to get to the bottom of it, then this is for you. All ages, demographics and levels
      of health and fitness are welcome.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What is included?</strong> A detailed verbal investigation, physical assessment,
      structural analysis, warm up massage, joint adjustment, muscle compensation correction and a
      rehab plan.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What do you need to know?</strong> Treatments are health fund rebatable for those that
      have chiropractic care cover as an extra. This can be claimed immediately at payment or post
      payment with an invoice. Patient gowns are provided although comfortable and mobile pants are
      recommended. Treatment modalities can be declined if you do not wish to use them although this
      may alter results. I will always discuss your treatment options and obtain informed consent
      before proceeding with a technique or modality.
    </p>
    <BookCta slug="new-patient" label="Book an initial consultation" />
  </article>

  <article class="consult">
    <h3>Follow-up consultation – 30 minutes, $95</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Who is this for?</strong> If you have already seen me in an initial consult in the past
      and are returning for further treatment of a new or old condition, problem or injury.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What is included?</strong> These consults start with a warm up massage where I get up to
      date on your progress since last session. This is followed by a reassessment of your
      musculoskeletal structure and a retesting of any joint compensations. We then proceed to joint
      adjustments and active muscle release or dry needling to address any current dysfunction in your
      body. We then finish with a rehab plan update if this is needed.
    </p>
    <BookCta slug="existing-patient" label="Book a follow-up consultation" />
  </article>

  {/* D1 — the Movement Plan Analysis is NEW to the website. It exists in
      Cliniko and is absent from the current site entirely. */}
  <article class="consult">
    <h3>Movement Plan Analysis – 1 hour, $160</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Who is this for?</strong> If you are not in pain right now but you want to understand
      your body’s limitations and build a plan around them. This is the dedicated version of the
      mobility work above: a full analysis session that produces your personalised routine.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How does it work?</strong> I will analyse and treat your body for any joint or muscle
      dysfunction using chiropractic skills to create the best environment for mobility progress. We
      will then test different flexibility positions which I will use to formulate the most efficient
      mobility routine for you to follow and increase your range of motion.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What you need to know.</strong> Health funds with chiropractic cover included can claim a
      rebate at time of payment or afterwards through an invoice. Comfortable and non-restrictive
      clothing such as active wear is required.
    </p>
    <BookCta slug="movement-plan-analysis" label="Book a Movement Plan Analysis" />
  </article>

  <Faq entries={TREATMENT_FAQ} />

  {/* G-9 — internal links to the Book page and between related services. */}
  <h2>Where to next?</h2>
  <ul class="next-links">
    <li><a href="/contact/">Book an appointment</a>: all booking types, including group sessions.</li>
    <li><a href="/services/">Services and pricing</a>: everything at a glance.</li>
    <li><a href="/services/group-classes/">Group classes</a>: a lower-commitment way to start.</li>
    <li><a href="/about/">About Keegan</a>: background, training and approach.</li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lead {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--ml-ink);
  }
  h3 {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  /* T6, Keegan 2026-08-06: the consult-type buttons "do not visually belong to
     their block of text".

     He is describing a spacing fact. The three types were a flat run of
     h3 / p / p / p / button, so each button sat the same distance from its own
     last paragraph as from the NEXT type's heading, and nothing said which one
     it belonged to. Grouping is the fix, not more spacing: each type is now one
     panel, so the heading, its copy and its button are visibly one object and
     the boundary between types is a real edge.

     ⚠️ THE ORANGE LEFT EDGE IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT DECORATION. The shade fill
     measures only 1.14 against paper, which is nowhere near enough to draw a
     panel on its own; the border is what makes the grouping visible, and
     removing it would quietly undo the fix while leaving the tint behind. That
     is a BORDER, which is what the accent token is for — never a text colour
     here. Measured: ink on shade 10.84 (text bar 4.5), orange on paper 3.22
     against the 3.0 non-text bar of WCAG 1.4.11 — headroom 0.22, so treat the
     accent hex as a floor here exactly as the book pill does. The pill itself
     is unaffected: its contrast is internal, paper label on its own orange
     fill, independent of the ground it sits on.

     `consider accompanying photos per type` is the other half of his note and
     is NOT done: it needs three photographs chosen by him, one per type. The
     panel is the right container for them when they arrive. */
  .consult {
    background: var(--ml-shade);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--ml-accent);
    border-radius: 0 14px 14px 0;
    padding: 0.25rem clamp(1.1rem, 3vw, 1.6rem) 1.25rem;
    margin-block: 1.75rem;
  }
  /* The panel's own top padding is the separation now, so the global 2rem that
     spaces headings in the flat run would double it. */
  .consult h3 {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }
  .consult > :last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .next-links {
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }
  .next-links li {
    margin: 0.35rem 0;
  }
</style>
